PythonC++ <pre><code>model = ssqpy.model.Model(
    horizon=50,
    dt=0.01,
    urdf_path="robot.urdf",
    free_flyer=True,
)
model.finalize(stage_cost=my_stage_cost, terminal_cost=my_terminal_cost)
</code></pre> <pre><code>#include "model/Model.hpp"

ssqp::model::Model::Structure structure;
structure.urdf_path  = "robot.urdf";
structure.free_flyer = true;

ssqp::model::Model model(50, 0.01, structure);

ssqp::model::Model::Config config;
config.stage_cost    = std::make_shared&lt;MyStageCost&gt;();
config.terminal_cost = std::make_shared&lt;MyTerminalCost&gt;();
model.finalize(config);
</code></pre> <p>See Model for the full set of constructor and <code>finalize</code> arguments.</p> <p>If your robot is one of the ones ssqp already bundles (Go2, A1, Panda, G1, Talos, or the double pendulum), you can skip the file entirely and pass <code>from_preset</code> instead; see Built-in presets.</p>

PythonC++ <pre><code>solver = ssqpy.solvers.ssqp(model)
state, x, u = solver.solve()

x.print()
u.print()
</code></pre> <pre><code>#include "solvers/ssqp.hpp"

ssqp::solvers::ssqp solver(model);

ssqp::solvers::ssqp::Guess guess(model.dims(), model.linear_dims());
ssqp::SolverState state = solver.solve(guess);

std::cout &lt;&lt; guess.x &lt;&lt; std::endl;
std::cout &lt;&lt; guess.u &lt;&lt; std::endl;
</code></pre> <p>See The SSQP Solver for termination criteria, line search and QP solver selection.</p>

PythonC++ <pre><code>mpc = ssqpy.solvers.MPC(model, sqp_iters=5, qp_iters=50)
mpc.reinitialize(initial_state)

while running:
    state = read_state()
    x, u = mpc.step(state)
    apply_controls(u.stage(0))
</code></pre> <pre><code>#include "solvers/mpc.hpp"

ssqp::solvers::MPC::Params mpc_params;
mpc_params.sqp_iters = 5;
mpc_params.qp_iters  = 50;

ssqp::solvers::MPC mpc(model, mpc_params);
mpc.reinitialize(initial_state);

while (running)
{
    VecX state  = read_state();
    auto [x, u] = mpc.step(state);
    apply_controls(u.stage(0));
}
</code></pre> <p>See MPC for warm-starting details and how <code>sqp_iters</code>/<code>qp_iters</code> bound per-step solve time.</p>

PythonC++ <pre><code>model.addContactConstraint(k, "foot_frame", mu=0.6, max_normal_force=200.0)
</code></pre> <pre><code>#include "model/stagewise/predefined/Constraints.hpp"

namespace constraints = ssqp::model::stagewise::predefined::constraints;

model.addConstraints&lt;constraints::ContactConstraint&gt;(k, model, "foot_frame", 0.6, 200.0);
</code></pre> <p>The contact constraint restricts the contact force (friction cone), keeps the foot from penetrating the ground and, by default, bounds the foot's linear velocity to zero so that a planted foot does not slide along the ground. Pass <code>no_slip=False</code> to drop that velocity bound, e.g. when you want to bound or penalize the foot's motion yourself instead.</p> <p>In C++, the templated <code>addConstraints&lt;C&gt;</code> automatically creates and wires up the force the contact needs (it reads <code>required_forces()</code>/<code>force_frame()</code> off the constraint); this is exactly the convenience the Python <code>addContactConstraint</code> wraps. See Contacts and forces in Model.</p>

PythonC++ <pre><code>model = ssqpy.model.Model(
    horizon, dt,
    solver_mode=ssqpy.model.SolverMode.InverseDynamics,
    integration=ssqpy.model.Integration.SemiImplicitEuler,
)
</code></pre> <pre><code>ssqp::model::Model::Structure structure;
structure.solver_mode = ssqp::InverseDynamics;
structure.integration = ssqp::SemiImplicitEuler;

ssqp::model::Model model(horizon, dt, structure);
</code></pre> <p>See Solver mode and Integration scheme in Model for what each option actually changes.</p>

PythonC++ <pre><code>data = solver.getConvergenceData()
data.print()
</code></pre> <pre><code>const auto &amp;data = solver.convergenceData();
data.print();
</code></pre> <p><code>ConvergenceData</code>/<code>ssqpConvergenceData</code> includes per-iteration norms, step size and stagewise cost/gap/violation breakdowns. See Termination in The SSQP Solver for what to look at first (e.g. <code>first_violation_stage</code>, <code>norm_dx</code>/<code>norm_du</code> not decreasing, or <code>max_iter_reached</code>).</p>

PythonC++ <pre><code>my_cost.set_enabled(False)
model.updateDimensions()  # only needed if a constraint's enabled state changed
</code></pre> <pre><code>my_cost-&gt;set_enabled(false);
model.updateDimensions();
</code></pre> <p>Toggle the object you added to the model (the one <code>addCost</code>/<code>addConstraints</code> was given or returned). The entries in <code>model.costs()</code>/<code>model.constraints()</code> are merged combinations of everything on a stage, and calling <code>set_enabled</code> on one throws.</p> <ul> <li>Costs: nothing more is needed, since disabling a cost doesn't change any dimension. A stage always keeps at   least one cost, so disabling every cost on a stage has no effect.</li> <li>Constraints: disabling one removes its rows from the stage's constraint vector, which changes the sizes in   <code>model.dims()</code>/<code>model.linear_dims()</code>. Call <code>updateDimensions</code> after toggling a constraint on a finalized model, and   construct a new solver afterwards, since a solver sizes its buffers when it is created. <code>solvers.MPC</code> does both for   you: <code>step()</code> calls <code>updateDimensions</code> and rebuilds the solver only if the sizes changed. Toggling before   <code>finalize</code> needs neither.</li> </ul>

